sections.body:
Consider a class that needs to send a notification whenever an order ships:

```java
public class OrderService {
    private final EmailNotifier notifier = new EmailNotifier();

    public void ship(Order order) {
        // ... shipping logic ...
        notifier.send(order.getCustomerEmail(), "Your order has shipped!");
    }
}
```

This compiles, runs, and looks perfectly reasonable at a glance. But look at what `OrderService` has taken on: it is not just responsible for *using* an email notifier, it is also responsible for *deciding which concrete implementation to construct* and *how to construct it*. Those are two very different responsibilities glued together. If you ever want to send an SMS instead of an email in some deployments, or swap in a fake notifier for a unit test, or point at a different SMTP configuration in staging versus production, you have to go edit `OrderService`'s source code -- because the `new EmailNotifier()` call is baked directly into it. `OrderService` is *tightly coupled* to one specific implementation, and that coupling is invisible to anyone calling `OrderService` from the outside; you'd only discover it by reading the class's internals.

Dependency Injection (DI) is the specific technique Spring uses to achieve IoC: dependencies are *passed into* an object from the outside, rather than *constructed inside* it. There are three ways to inject a dependency in Spring -- constructor injection, setter injection, and field injection -- but they all share the same underlying idea. Rewriting the example above with constructor injection and an interface:

```java
public interface Notifier {
    void send(String recipient, String message);
}

@Component
public class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String recipient, String message) {
        // ... actually send an email ...
    }
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final Notifier notifier;

    public OrderService(Notifier notifier) {
        this.notifier = notifier;
    }

    public void ship(Order order) {
        notifier.send(order.getCustomerEmail(), "Your order has shipped!");
    }
}
```

`OrderService` no longer knows or cares that `EmailNotifier` exists. It only knows about the `Notifier` interface. Spring's container sees that `OrderService` needs *something* implementing `Notifier`, finds the `EmailNotifier` bean it already created, and passes it into the constructor automatically when it builds the `OrderService` bean. Nothing inside `OrderService` changed to make this swappable -- the swappability was there the moment it stopped calling `new` itself.

