sections.body:
`@Component` is the generic, foundational stereotype annotation: 'this class should be scanned and registered as a bean.' `@Service`, `@Repository`, and `@Controller` are all, themselves, annotated with `@Component` -- meaning they are specializations of it, and from the container's point of view, purely as far as *bean registration* goes, they behave identically to a plain `@Component`. So why bother distinguishing them at all?

First, semantic clarity: seeing `@Repository` on a class immediately tells a reader 'this talks to a data store,' and `@Service` says 'this holds business logic,' which is genuinely useful documentation that a bare `@Component` doesn't provide. Second, and more concretely, `@Repository` triggers real additional behavior: Spring registers a `PersistenceExceptionTranslationPostProcessor` that wraps `@Repository`-annotated beans so that low-level, persistence-technology-specific exceptions (a JPA `PersistenceException`, for example) get automatically translated into Spring's own unified, unchecked `DataAccessException` hierarchy -- meaning your service layer can catch one consistent family of exceptions regardless of which persistence technology sits underneath. Third, some tooling and AOP pointcuts are written to specifically target `@Service` or `@Repository` rather than the generic `@Component`, so using the wrong stereotype can silently exclude a bean from behavior that's supposed to apply to it. In short: functionally almost identical for plain wiring, but not truly interchangeable once you look past the basics.

```java
@Repository
public class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    // ... persistence-specific exceptions thrown here get translated automatically
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repository;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}
```

