Each autoconfiguration class is guarded by one or more conditional annotations that decide, at startup, whether it activates. `@ConditionalOnClass(SomeClass.class)` checks whether a specific class is actually present on the classpath -- `JacksonAutoConfiguration`, for instance, is guarded by a check for Jackson's `ObjectMapper` class, so it only activates at all if some starter (typically `spring-boot-starter-web` or `-json`) has actually put Jackson on the classpath. `@ConditionalOnProperty` gates activation on a specific configuration property being set (or set to a particular value) in `application.properties`/`.yml`.

The one that matters most for everyday development is `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`. Spring Boot's own default beans -- the embedded Tomcat factory, the default `ObjectMapper`, and dozens of others -- are deliberately annotated `@ConditionalOnMissingBean(TheType.class)`, meaning: 'only register this default if no bean of this type already exists in the context.' This is the designed escape hatch that lets you override any autoconfigured default cleanly: define your own `@Bean` of that type, anywhere in your own configuration, and Boot's default politely backs off instead of colliding with it. There is no conflict, no ambiguous-bean error -- that is exactly the intended behavior, not a special case to work around defensively.

```java
@Configuration
public class JacksonConfig {
    @Bean
    public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        mapper.registerModule(new JavaTimeModule());
        return mapper;
    }
}
```

Because this `ObjectMapper` bean now exists in the context, `JacksonAutoConfiguration`'s own `@ConditionalOnMissingBean(ObjectMapper.class)`-guarded default bean method simply does not run at all -- your custom mapper, with its extra module registered, is the one every controller's JSON conversion ends up using.

examples.code:
@Configuration
public class JacksonConfig {

    @Bean
    public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        mapper.registerModule(new JavaTimeModule());
        mapper.disable(SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS);
        return mapper;
    }
}

